Hi Finn & Elias,

I’m using Haakon’s barrier approach in the LGCP model and so, as Finn mentioned, can’t currently fit my model with inlabru.

Elias, do you have an example of how to structure a space-time LGCP model without aggregating the data?

Many thanks,

James

> On 13 Nov 2019, at 14:33, Finn Lindgren <finn.l...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> Note: inlabru doesn’t yet support rgeneric models properly, so while lgcp models are easier with inlabru, one cannot at the moment combine them with barrier-SPDE models. I aim to sort that out in the development version soon.
>
> Finn
>
>> On 13 Nov 2019, at 14:29, Elias T. Krainski <eliask...@gmail.com> wrote:
>>
>> ﻿
>>>
>>> Sec 8.4 aggregates the points into counts for a dual mesh, which turns the continuous Point process model into a discrete count model, via a zero-order integration approximation. The general LGCP likelihood approximation doesn’t do that.
>>
>> This example in the book was motivated to face issues with large data. At the time it was written we haven't yet the 'adaptive' strategy available in INLA. Now, one can avoid the 'binding' approach of aggregating data into dual mesh polygons for large point process data by considering control.inla(strategy='adaptive'). Furthermore, the examples on LGCP in the book and fancier ones can be easier done with the inlabru package.
>>
>> Elias
>>
>>
